# Session Log

Cross-machine continuity log for Claude Code sessions on this project.
This file is tracked in git so it can be synced between laptops (commit + push / pull).

Newest entries at the top. Each entry: date, machine (optional), summary of
what was done, decisions made, and what's next.

---

## 2026-09-26 (later)
- Root-caused a real bug the user hit: `runTlsPub.bat --qos=control`
  failed with "Unknown flag: --qos" (no "=control" in the error text).
  Cause: cmd.exe treats space, comma, semicolon, AND "=" as parameter
  delimiters for UNQUOTED batch args -- `--qos=control` arrives at the
  script as TWO tokens, "--qos" and "control", with the "=" silently
  dropped, before our script's own parsing ever runs. This affects
  EVERY "--flag=value" in the project's .bat files, not just --qos.
- Fixed in `runTlsPub.bat`: --topic/--qos/--priority/--congestion/
  --node-id/--express/--auto-timestamp now all accept both the
  cmd.exe-split two-token form ("--flag" "value") and the
  quoted-single-token form ("--flag=value"), normalizing back to a
  single flag before forwarding to Java. Documented the gotcha in both
  the REM header and the --help printout.
- Found the SAME bug in `runPub.bat` (forwards `%*` raw, no parsing at
  all -- its own documented examples like `--priority=real_time` would
  hit it too). NOT fixed yet -- reported to the user, awaiting their
  call on whether to fix it there as well.
- Verified with `mvn -q package`; hand-traced the parser against the
  user's failing case plus all documented examples (still can't exec
  cmd.exe directly from this sandboxed Bash tool).

## 2026-09-26
- Added `--qos=<name>` and a `QOS` preset variable (default `data`) to
  `runTlsPub.bat`. Clarified with the user first: `--qos` is a
  priority-name alias for `--priority` (not a composite
  priority+congestion+express value).
  Precedence: `--qos` > `--priority` > `QOS` default. Both `--qos=`
  and `--priority=` are now intercepted in the batch file (Pass 1
  `:scanflags` + Pass 2 skip) and re-emitted as a single explicit
  `--priority=<resolved>` flag, so the Java sample never sees two
  conflicting `--priority` flags or an unknown `--qos` one.
  `QOS=data` composes to `Qos.DEFAULT` (value-equal, per
  `Qos.isDefault()`), so the wire output is unchanged from before this
  feature existed when QOS is left at its default.
  Listed all 8 valid priority names in both the REM header and the
  `--help` printout, per the user's ask.
  No Java changes needed — --qos ultimately flows through the existing
  --priority mechanism, which already gets embedded in the published
  payload (see below).
- Verified with `mvn -q package`; hand-traced the parser (couldn't
  exec cmd.exe directly in this sandboxed Bash tool).
- Updated README's mTLS publisher section accordingly.

## 2026-09-25 (later still)
- Embedded the resolved QoS into each published payload in
  `ZenohJavaTlsPub.java` (e.g. `hello #1 from pure-Java (mTLS)
  [qos=DEFAULT (no ext)]`), so a subscriber sees what QoS a message
  was sent with, not just the publisher's console banner. Reused the
  same `qosLabel` string for both the banner and the payload.
  Verified with `mvn -q package`.
- Updated README's mTLS publisher section to mention this.
- Committed and pushed all outstanding work from this session
  (--topic flag + QoS-in-payload + doc updates).

## 2026-09-25 (later)
- Added `--topic=<keyExpr>` support to `runTlsPub.bat`, plus a batch-level
  `TOPIC` default (`996dfb6c880346559dff117458d27b66/zenoh-client/test-topic`).
- Rewrote the arg-handling in `runTlsPub.bat`: replaced the old
  `:no_args`/`:one_arg`/`:two_args`/`:three_or_more_args` positional
  dispatch with a two-pass parser (a non-destructive scan for
  `--topic=`, then a shift-loop that fills keyExpr/count/interval or
  just count/interval when `--topic` is present). This was necessary
  because `--topic` can appear anywhere in the args and must not be
  forwarded to the Java sample (it has no `--topic` flag of its own).
  Precedence: `--topic` flag > positional keyExpr > `TOPIC` default.
  Verified by hand-tracing the parser logic (couldn't exec cmd.exe
  directly from this sandboxed Bash tool to test interactively).
- Updated README.md's "mTLS publisher with QoS" section and the
  in-file REM/help text to document the new flag and precedence rule.
- Not yet committed/pushed — waiting on user confirmation.

